#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204629} :activity/id #uuid "e0af35b8-017c-57d5-b816-3ea43a458e76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050335 :topic/id #uuid "40d51ec6-50e2-56a3-b67d-694483590810" :topic/math-academy-id 4822} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205242 :tutorial/content "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is **transitive** if, for all $x,y,z \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y}) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z}) \\in R\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $({\\color{blue}x},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y})$ and $(\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{y},{\\color{blue}z})$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $({\\color{blue}x},{\\color{blue}z})$ should also belong to the relation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The relation \"greater than\" ($\\gt$), defined over $\\mathbb{R}$, is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\gt {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n- The relation \"are congruent modulo $n$\" ($\\equiv$), defined over $\\mathbb{Z}$, is transitive since ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ implies ${\\color{blue}a} \\equiv {\\color{blue}c}$ for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$, where $n$ is a fixed natural number.\n\nNotice from the definition of a transitive relation that if the first ordered pair is $(y,y)$, we have\n$(y,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (y,z) \\in R$\nwhich is always true.\n\nSimilarly, if the second ordered pair is $(y,y)$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,y) \\in R$\nwhich is always true.\n\nTherefore, to verify transitivity, it's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $(y,z)$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "7ff5ebb7-d081-52f4-b3a5-c24c7fe5d202" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9081 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "817ab16b-a46d-5955-8708-cb1f47546f3e" :step/math-academy-id 136768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203707 :question/id #uuid "c802160b-40ba-5bef-a32b-010ebdc4851f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14665"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004a102d-cfb9-539a-9fdd-11ca604fd7ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204266}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations"} :step/id #uuid "1762f4ff-dc88-5b0c-9805-520fa3b43039" :step/math-academy-id 136769 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205248 :tutorial/content "Let's consider some \"nuanced\" cases.\n\n- For any set $A$, the universal relation $R = A^2$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n- The empty relation $R = \\emptyset$ is transitive. Since it does not contain any pairs, the antecedent in the implication from the definition of transitivity is always false:\n$\\underbrace{(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,z) \\in R}_{\\large \\textrm{false}} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (x,z) \\in R$\nTherefore, the implication is always true, and the relation is vacuously transitive.\n- The relation $R = \\{(1,2), (3,4)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs. So, the antecedent in the implication is always false (similar to the empty case above), and therefore the implication is always true.\n- The relation $R=\\{(1,1), (2,2)\\}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs (i.e., it contains only reflexive pairs)." :tutorial/id #uuid "517981b7-195e-5fc0-9683-e0125c43d8d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9109 :tutorial/title "Special Cases"} :step/id #uuid "b79bb19d-072b-5a55-b055-490dc5d96a87" :step/math-academy-id 136772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203718 :question/id #uuid "b2641213-6efe-5412-a585-9cb348ab00b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14701"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048805}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204293}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"} :step/id #uuid "0cd41e4a-47e4-57bb-a21b-546af8fcc1ec" :step/math-academy-id 136773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205253 :tutorial/content "A binary relation is transitive if and only if for every pair of \"linked\" arrows $x \\rightarrow y$ and $y \\rightarrow z$ in the corresponding digraph, the arrow $x \\rightarrow z$ also belongs to the graph. For example, the relation represented by the following digraph is transitive.\n\n![](images/96/96fe8200e2ebd03c23ade6b649a41345f75b66524c188d419ee12ea3c904942b.png)\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" arrows of the form $x \\to y$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nand $y \\to z$ with\n\n$$\ny \\neq z{:}\n$$\n\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 2$ that ends at $2$. It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $2{:}$ Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 1$ requires that $1 \\to 1$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$Linking $1 \\to 2$ with $2 \\to 3$ requires that $1 \\to 3$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- Consider the arrow $2 \\to 1$ that ends at $1$. It can be linked with the following arrows that starts at $1{:}$ Linking $2 \\to 1$ with $1 \\to 2$ requires that $2 \\to 2$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$Linking $2 \\to 1$ with $1 \\to 3$ requires that $2 \\to 3$ belongs to the graph, and this arrow is in the graph $\\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- Consider the arrow $1 \\to 3$ that ends at $3$. It can't be linked with any arrow since none of them start at $3$.\n- Consider the arrow $2 \\to 3$ that ends at $3$. It can't be linked with any arrow since none of them start at $3$.\n\nOn the other hand, the relation that is represented by the digraph below is not transitive since the digraph has arrows $1 \\to 2$ and $2 \\to 4$ but has no arrow $1 \\to 4$.\n\n![](images/04/04ade4abe648bcbd790c6355461fb3f74184667a0a6f4fbbafa8b84859c8c28f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "60c38e6a-6089-5c3b-ad1a-592edfe5d5d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9118 :tutorial/title "Digraphs Representing Transitive Relations"} :step/id #uuid "dab3ff3f-5ed9-5e06-b684-c1e8180229d8" :step/math-academy-id 136776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203708 :question/id #uuid "7a9c02ab-eae3-52ee-9dd4-c81ca204ad4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14666"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048710}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204269}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"} :step/id #uuid "39d5f554-2423-5519-95c8-1637a442da03" :step/math-academy-id 136777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203709 :question/id #uuid "9e7589ef-e172-57ee-84f3-e085724db718" :question/math-academy-id "e-14667"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050176}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204271}] :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "8460f65a-2290-5c97-87a5-237ff636191c" :step/math-academy-id 136780}] :activity/title "Transitive Relations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204114}]}
